videocodec.library/CloseVideoStream        videocodec.library/CloseVideoStream

   NAME
	CloseVideoStream -- Closes a stream.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn CloseVideoStream(base: *VideoCodecBase, stream: ?*vc.VideoStream) void

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -24.

   INPUTS
	- `stream`: what OpenVideoStream made, or null.

   RESULT
	None.

   BEHAVIOR
	The subtitles' process is told to end and waited for (SINCE 1.3), the
	sound's stream is closed, the decoder of the pictures with every
	frame still lent, the film - and the file, when the stream opened it
	(VCA_Name) - and the libraries the stream opened.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, on the file and the chip.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held.
	- Process: a process when it closes a file it opened.

   OWNERSHIP
	The stream is gone after the call, and every frame it lent with it:
	none may be shown after. Neither half may be being decoded while it
	closes. A handle the program gave (VCA_FileHandle) is still the
	program's.

   NOTES
	None.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`OpenVideoStream`

   EXAMPLES
	    vcb.CloseVideoStream(film);
videocodec.library/DecodeVideoFrame        videocodec.library/DecodeVideoFrame

   NAME
	DecodeVideoFrame -- Hands out the next frame in the order frames are shown.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn DecodeVideoFrame(base: *VideoCodecBase, stream: *vc.VideoStream, frame: *vc.VideoFrame) i32

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -32.

   INPUTS
	- `stream`: what OpenVideoStream made.
	- `frame`: where the frame goes.

   RESULT
	VCERR_OK and the frame in `frame`; VCERR_END when every frame has been
	handed out; VCERR_BUSY with VIDEO_HELD_FRAMES frames held; VCERR_CORRUPT
	for a picture the decoder stopped on - called again, it goes on with
	the next; VCERR_UNSUPPORTED, VCERR_READ, VCERR_NOMEMORY.

   BEHAVIOR
	The picture track's packets are decoded until the next frame comes
	out: the codec module's decoder is sent them a packet at a time while
	it wants more, and hands out its frames in the order they are shown -
	the chip's fed a picture at a time, a Motion JPEG picture unpacked into
	its planes. A frame due before what the film presents, or before the
	key frame a seek landed on, is passed over. At the end of the track the
	decoder hands out the frames it still holds back, and then VCERR_END.
	A decoder that refuses the stream before its first frame - the chip
	and 10-bit H.264 - gives way to the next module that decodes the codec,
	which starts at the key frame again (SINCE 1.4; not for a film read
	through a function). The frame's `time` is when it is due, and `number`
	counts the frames handed out since the stream was opened or moved.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, on the file and the chip.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held.
	- Process: a process for a file; a task will do with VCA_ReadFunc.

   OWNERSHIP
	The frame's planes are the decoder's, lent until ReleaseVideoFrame
	gives them back; they do not change while lent. The pictures are
	decoded by one task at a time, which may be another than the one that
	decodes the sound.

   NOTES
	The chip decodes 1080p H.264 at about 150 frames a second. Motion JPEG
	is decoded on the calling task, about a second for a 1080p picture on
	the VisionFive 2: for small pictures.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`ReleaseVideoFrame`, `SeekVideo`, graphics' `WriteYUVPixelArray`

   EXAMPLES
	    var frame: vc.VideoFrame = .{};
	    while (vcb.DecodeVideoFrame(film, &frame) == vc.VCERR_OK) {
	        waitUntil(frame.time);
	        _ = gfx.WriteYUVPixelArray(rp, &frame.picture, 0, 0, &.{ .x = 0, .y = 0, .w = frame.width, .h = frame.height });
	        vcb.ReleaseVideoFrame(film, &frame);
	    }
videocodec.library/DecodeVideoSound        videocodec.library/DecodeVideoSound

   NAME
	DecodeVideoSound -- Hands out the film's sound as 16-bit samples, as DecodeAudio does.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn DecodeVideoSound(base: *VideoCodecBase, stream: *vc.VideoStream, buffer: [*]i16, frames: u32) i32

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -40.

   INPUTS
	- `stream`: what OpenVideoStream made.
	- `buffer`: room for `frames` frames: `frames` times the channels the
	  info gives, 16-bit samples, the channels interleaved.
	- `frames`: how many frames are wanted.

   RESULT
	How many frames it put there - as many as asked until the end - 0 at
	the end of the sound, or below 0: VCERR_NOSOUND for a film without
	sound, VCERR_CORRUPT, VCERR_READ, VCERR_NOMEMORY.

   BEHAVIOR
	audiocodec.library's stream on the film's sound track decodes it: the
	samples are those DecodeAudio hands out, at the rate and in the
	channels GetVideoStreamInfo says, the encoder's priming left out, so
	the first frame is the one at the start of what the film presents.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, on the file.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held.
	- Process: a process: the file is read.

   OWNERSHIP
	`buffer` is the caller's. The sound is decoded by one task at a time,
	which may be another than the one that decodes the pictures, at the
	same time.

   NOTES
	None.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`DecodeVideoFrame`, `SeekVideo`, audiocodec's `DecodeAudio`

   EXAMPLES
	    var samples: [2048 * 2]i16 = undefined;
	    const got = vcb.DecodeVideoSound(film, &samples, 2048);
videocodec.library/GetVideoStreamAttrs  videocodec.library/GetVideoStreamAttrs

   NAME
	GetVideoStreamAttrs -- Answers media.library's tags for a film: its names, its cover, its chapters.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn GetVideoStreamAttrs(base: *VideoCodecBase, stream: *vc.VideoStream, tags: ?[*]const utility.TagItem) u32

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -56.

   INPUTS
	- `stream`: what OpenVideoStream made.
	- `tags`: media.library's `MDA_*` tags, each with where its answer
	  goes.

   RESULT
	How many of the tags were answered.

   BEHAVIOR
	They are media.library's GetMediaAttrs on the film, as it is: the
	title, the artist and the rest at full length, the year, the chapters.
	A cover is not kept: `MDA_Picture` answers null.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none.
	- Process: a task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	Texts and the chapters are the film's, good until the stream is
	closed.

   NOTES
	None.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`GetVideoStreamInfo`, media.library's `GetMediaAttrs`

   EXAMPLES
	    var chapters: u32 = 0;
	    _ = vcb.GetVideoStreamAttrs(film, &[_]utility.TagItem{
	        .{ .tag = media.MDA_Chapters, .data = @intFromPtr(&chapters) },
	        .{},
	    });
videocodec.library/GetVideoStreamInfo    videocodec.library/GetVideoStreamInfo

   NAME
	GetVideoStreamInfo -- Says what a film is: its pictures, its length, its sound, its title.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn GetVideoStreamInfo(base: *VideoCodecBase, stream: *vc.VideoStream, info: *vc.VideoStreamInfo) void

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -28.

   INPUTS
	- `stream`: what OpenVideoStream made.
	- `info`: where it goes.

   RESULT
	None; `info` is filled in.

   BEHAVIOR
	The pictures' codec and size and the number of them come from the
	film's index; their rate from it too - the frames a second on average,
	to a thousandth - or else from the stream, once the decoder has read
	it. Where the decoder has read the stream, its size as shown - cropped
	- is the one given. The length is the picture track's,
	or the film's. The sound is audiocodec's stream on the film's sound
	track: its codec, rate and channels, as DecodeVideoSound hands it out.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none.
	- Process: a task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	`info` is the caller's.

   NOTES
	None.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`OpenVideoStream`, `GetVideoStreamAttrs`

   EXAMPLES
	    var info: vc.VideoStreamInfo = .{};
	    vcb.GetVideoStreamInfo(film, &info);
videocodec.library/GetVideoSubtitle        videocodec.library/GetVideoSubtitle

   NAME
	GetVideoSubtitle -- Says what the chosen subtitles show at a time: lines of text, or a picture.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn GetVideoSubtitle(base: *VideoCodecBase, stream: *vc.VideoStream, time: i64, subtitle: *vc.VideoSubtitle) i32

   SINCE
	1.3. LVO -68.

   INPUTS
	- `stream`: what OpenVideoStream made.
	- `time`: the time of the frame being shown, in microseconds.
	- `subtitle`: where what shows goes.

   RESULT
	VCERR_OK, `subtitle` filled in; or VCERR_END when nothing shows - no
	subtitles chosen, none at this time - and `subtitle` is cleared.

   BEHAVIOR
	The subtitles are read ahead of the film by a process of the
	library's, so the call does not wait on the file: a subtitle not yet
	read - just after a seek, on a slow disk - is not shown; after a seek
	they are read from five seconds before, for the lines still showing.
	Text is
	UTF-8, its lines apart by `\n`: SubRip's tags and SubStation Alpha's
	codes are taken out, and of SubStation Alpha only the dialogue is kept
	- signs placed on the picture and drawings are left out. Lines that
	show at once come one under another, in the order they began;
	VSUB_ITALIC says that all of it is in italics. A Blu-ray's picture
	(PGS) comes as `pixels`, fitted to the film's size where the disc's
	is another, and shows until the next; a picture is handed out before
	text. `start` and `end` say from when up to when it shows the same:
	a program draws it again only when they change. `end` is
	`std.math.maxInt(i64)` while the track has not said.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no - but a stream whose subtitles could not get a process of
	  their own reads the file here.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held.
	- Process: a task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	`subtitle` is the caller's; the text and the pixels are the library's,
	good until the call is made again, SeekVideo or SelectVideoTrack moves
	the film, or it is closed. Called by the task that decodes the
	pictures.

   NOTES
	Asked about each frame shown, as a player does, it reads ahead
	ten seconds at most.

   BUGS
	A subtitle that began more than five seconds before the place a seek
	lands on is missed there.

   SEE ALSO
	`SelectVideoTrack`, `GetVideoTrack`, `VCA_Subtitles`

   EXAMPLES
	    var shown: vc.VideoSubtitle = .{};
	    if (vcb.GetVideoSubtitle(film, frame.time, &shown) == vc.VCERR_OK and
	        (shown.start != last.start or shown.end != last.end)) drawSubtitle(&shown);
videocodec.library/GetVideoTrack              videocodec.library/GetVideoTrack

   NAME
	GetVideoTrack -- Says what one of a film's tracks is, whether something here decodes it, and whether it is the one being played or shown.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn GetVideoTrack(base: *VideoCodecBase, stream: *vc.VideoStream, number: u32, track: *media.MediaTrack) i32

   SINCE
	1.3. LVO -60.

   INPUTS
	- `stream`: what OpenVideoStream made.
	- `number`: the track, from 0, as media.library's GetMediaTrack
	  numbers them.
	- `track`: where what it is goes.

   RESULT
	0 or more, its flags: `VTRK_DECODED` - the chip or the library
	decodes its pictures, audiocodec.library its sound, the library its
	subtitles - and `VTRK_CHOSEN` - its pictures are being decoded, its
	sound is DecodeVideoSound's, its subtitles GetVideoSubtitle's; or
	VCERR_END past the last track.

   BEHAVIOR
	`track` is filled in as GetMediaTrack fills it: its kind, codec,
	language and name. Of the pictures only the track being decoded is
	VTRK_DECODED. A sound track is when the stream has audiocodec.library
	- it was opened with sound, from a file - and the library's version
	decodes its codec: AAC since 1.6, MP3, FLAC and Vorbis since 1.7,
	AC-3 and E-AC-3 since 1.8. Subtitles are when they are SubRip's,
	SubStation Alpha's, timed text or a Blu-ray's pictures (PGS), and the
	stream was opened from a file.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none.
	- Process: a task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	`track` is the caller's; the setup and name it points to are the
	film's, good until the stream is closed.

   NOTES
	A program lists the tracks from 0 until VCERR_END - for a menu of the
	sound and the subtitles, by language and name.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`SelectVideoTrack`, `GetVideoSubtitle`, media.library's `GetMediaTrack`

   EXAMPLES
	    var number: u32 = 0;
	    var track: media.MediaTrack = .{};
	    while (true) : (number += 1) {
	        const flags = vcb.GetVideoTrack(film, number, &track);
	        if (flags < 0) break;
	        if (track.kind == media.MTRACK_AUDIO and flags & vc.VTRK_DECODED != 0) addSoundItem(number, &track);
	    }
videocodec.library/IdentifyVideo              videocodec.library/IdentifyVideo

   NAME
	IdentifyVideo -- Says what codec the pictures of a film are in, by the first bytes of its file.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn IdentifyVideo(base: *VideoCodecBase, bytes: [*]const u8, length: u32) u32

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -48.

   INPUTS
	- `bytes`, `length`: the file's first bytes - VIDEO_IDENTIFY_BYTES
	  are best.

   RESULT
	The codec of its first track of pictures, VCODEC_*; VCODEC_UNKNOWN when
	the bytes do not show it.

   BEHAVIOR
	The bytes are read as an MP4 - media.library's kind - box by box, to
	its index (`moov`) and in it the first track of pictures, whose first
	sample entry names the codec. An MP4 whose index lies behind the bytes
	given - at its end, as a film written in one go has it - shows
	nothing: VCODEC_UNKNOWN, though OpenVideoStream reads its index
	wherever it is. A Matroska file (SINCE 1.2) is read element by element
	to its tracks, which muxers put near the front, and the first track of
	pictures names the codec by its ID; VP8, VP9 and AV1 are
	VCODEC_UNKNOWN. media.library's IdentifyMedia says whether the bytes
	begin a film at all.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: yes.
	- Locks: none.
	- Process: a task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The bytes are the caller's, only read.

   NOTES
	A codec it names may still be one nothing here decodes: VCODEC_MPEG4V,
	VCODEC_VP9 and VCODEC_AV1, for which there is no module; H.265 where
	no module in LIBS:codecs/ decodes it - chip.vcodec on a board without
	the chip.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`OpenVideoStream`, media.library's `IdentifyMedia`

   EXAMPLES
	    const got = dl.Read(fh, &head, head.len);
	    if (got > 0 and vcb.IdentifyVideo(&head, @intCast(got)) == vc.VCODEC_H264) {
	        // a film for the chip
	    }
videocodec.library/OpenVideoStream          videocodec.library/OpenVideoStream

   NAME
	OpenVideoStream -- Opens a film and finds a decoder for its pictures and one for its sound - and reads its subtitles.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn OpenVideoStream(base: *VideoCodecBase, tags: ?[*]const utility.TagItem, err: ?*i32) ?*vc.VideoStream

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -20.

   INPUTS
	- `tags`: where the bytes come from - one of `VCA_Name` (a file to
	  open), `VCA_FileHandle` (a file the program has open, the film
	  beginning where it is) or `VCA_ReadFunc` with `VCA_ReadData` (a
	  function of the program's) - and `VCA_Sound` FALSE for the pictures
	  alone; `VCA_SoundTrack` and `VCA_Subtitles` choose the tracks
	  (SINCE 1.3).
	- `err`: where why it failed goes (VCERR_*), or null.

   RESULT
	The stream, or null - and `err` says why: VCERR_OPEN without a source,
	when the file would not open or media.library could not be;
	VCERR_UNKNOWN for bytes that are not a container media.library knows,
	or a film with no pictures; VCERR_NOCODEC when no codec module
	decodes its pictures here; VCERR_BUSY when the chip's decoders are all
	in use and no other module takes them;
	VCERR_UNSUPPORTED, VCERR_READ, VCERR_CORRUPT, VCERR_NOMEMORY.

   BEHAVIOR
	The film is opened with media.library, which finds out what it is and
	reads its index - an MP4's, a Matroska file's header (SINCE 1.2). Its
	first track of pictures - of a Matroska file the first it marks to be
	played - is taken, and a decoder for it found among the codec modules
	in LIBS:codecs/ whose tags name its codec (SINCE 1.4): a chip's before
	software, the higher priority first; the first that opens a decoder
	for the track decodes it - chip.vcodec H.264 and H.265 on the chip
	(videodec.resource), reading the track's parameter sets; mjpeg.vcodec
	Motion JPEG. Its first track of sound - in a Matroska file the
	marked one, or `VCA_SoundTrack`'s - is opened with audiocodec.library
	1.6 or later, on the same file (`ACA_Media`) - AAC, with 1.7 MP3, FLAC
	and Vorbis, with 1.8 AC-3 and E-AC-3 - unless `VCA_Sound` is FALSE or
	the film is read through a function. A sound track nothing decodes
	leaves the film without sound - `sound_codec` 0 in the info - rather
	than unopened. The subtitles the film marks to be shown - in a
	Matroska file the track marked forced and default, else default
	(media.library 1.3's `MDA_Subtitles`) - or `VCA_Subtitles`' are read
	from the start by a process of the library's, for GetVideoSubtitle
	(SINCE 1.3); none from a film read through a function. The first frame
	and the first samples are those at the start of what the film
	presents.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, on the file, LIBS:codecs/ and the chip.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held.
	- Process: a process: the file and the modules' tags are read, a
	  module is loaded, and the chip's decoder reads its firmware's file.

   OWNERSHIP
	The stream is the caller's, given back with `CloseVideoStream`. A file
	opened with VCA_Name is closed with it; a handle given with
	VCA_FileHandle stays the program's, and must stay open until then.

   NOTES
	It takes the film's index in memory (media.library's), the chip's
	stream buffer of 2 MiB or four of the largest packets - 8 MiB for a
	Matroska file, which says not its largest - and audiocodec's stream;
	Motion JPEG takes a frame's planes for each frame lent. A module's tag
	is read again only when its file changed; a module newer on the disk
	than in memory is loaded anew once no film has the old one. media.library
	reads a Matroska file's tracks ahead, 256 KiB at a time each.
	Subtitles take about 12 KiB, a process with a stack of 32 KiB, and
	what they read ahead: up to ten seconds of lines, or a picture.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`CloseVideoStream`, `GetVideoStreamInfo`, `DecodeVideoFrame`,
	`DecodeVideoSound`, `IdentifyVideo`, `GetVideoTrack`

   EXAMPLES
	    var err: i32 = 0;
	    const film = vcb.OpenVideoStream(&[_]utility.TagItem{
	        .{ .tag = vc.VCA_Name, .data = @intFromPtr("SD0:Films/trip.mp4") },
	        .{},
	    }, &err) orelse {
	        _ = Printf(dl, "%s\n", .{vcb.VideoErrorText(err)});
	        return;
	    };
	    defer vcb.CloseVideoStream(film);
videocodec.library/ReleaseVideoFrame      videocodec.library/ReleaseVideoFrame

   NAME
	ReleaseVideoFrame -- Gives a frame DecodeVideoFrame handed out back to the decoder.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn ReleaseVideoFrame(base: *VideoCodecBase, stream: *vc.VideoStream, frame: *vc.VideoFrame) void

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -36.

   INPUTS
	- `stream`: what OpenVideoStream made.
	- `frame`: a frame it handed out and not yet taken back.

   RESULT
	None.

   BEHAVIOR
	The frame's buffer goes back to the decoder, which decodes into it
	again, and `frame` is cleared. A frame that is not one the stream has
	lent - given back already, or cleared - is passed by.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: none.
	- Process: a task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The planes are the decoder's again after the call; the frame may not
	be shown after. Frames may be given back in any order, by the task
	that decodes the pictures.

   NOTES
	None.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`DecodeVideoFrame`

   EXAMPLES
	    vcb.ReleaseVideoFrame(film, &frame);
videocodec.library/SeekVideo                      videocodec.library/SeekVideo

   NAME
	SeekVideo -- Moves a film's pictures to the key frame at or before a time, and its sound to that frame's time.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn SeekVideo(base: *VideoCodecBase, stream: *vc.VideoStream, time: i64) i64

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -44.

   INPUTS
	- `stream`: what OpenVideoStream made.
	- `time`: where to, in microseconds from the start of what the film
	  presents.

   RESULT
	The time of the key frame it moved to, 0 or more; below 0 a VCERR_*:
	VCERR_NOSEEK for a film read through VCA_ReadFunc, VCERR_READ,
	VCERR_CORRUPT.

   BEHAVIOR
	The picture track is moved to the key frame at or before `time` - the
	first, before the first - where a decoder can start; the decoder
	drops what it was sent and not yet shown. The next frame
	DecodeVideoFrame hands out is that key frame, `number` 0: frames due
	before it are passed over. The sound is moved to the key frame's time,
	so the next samples DecodeVideoSound hands out go with it, and the
	subtitles are read from there (SINCE 1.3).

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, on the file - and for a piece of sound being decoded by
	  another task to be done.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held.
	- Process: a process: the file is read.

   OWNERSHIP
	Called by the task that decodes the pictures. The frames lent stay
	lent, and are given back as before.

   NOTES
	A film's key frames are often seconds apart; a program that wants a
	later frame decodes on to it, and shows from there.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`DecodeVideoFrame`, `DecodeVideoSound`

   EXAMPLES
	    const landed = vcb.SeekVideo(film, 60 * 1_000_000);
	    if (landed >= 0) clock.restartAt(landed);
videocodec.library/SelectVideoTrack        videocodec.library/SelectVideoTrack

   NAME
	SelectVideoTrack -- Chooses another of a film's sound tracks, or its subtitles - or none.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn SelectVideoTrack(base: *VideoCodecBase, stream: *vc.VideoStream, track: u32, time: i64) i32

   SINCE
	1.3. LVO -64.

   INPUTS
	- `stream`: what OpenVideoStream made.
	- `track`: a sound or a text track, numbered as GetVideoTrack numbers
	  them - or `VIDEO_NO_TRACK`, the subtitles off.
	- `time`: where the film is, in microseconds: the sound starts there,
	  and the subtitles are read from there.

   RESULT
	VCERR_OK; or VCERR_NOCODEC for a track nothing here decodes,
	VCERR_UNSUPPORTED for a track of pictures, VCERR_NOSOUND for sound on
	a stream opened without it, VCERR_NOSEEK for a film read through
	VCA_ReadFunc, VCERR_END for a track past the last, VCERR_NOMEMORY -
	and what was chosen stays.

   BEHAVIOR
	A sound track is opened with audiocodec.library on the film, moved to
	`time`, and takes the place of the one DecodeVideoSound decoded, which
	is closed: the next samples are the new track's, at its rate and in
	its channels, which GetVideoStreamInfo then says. A text track is read
	from `time` by the subtitles' process - started now if none was
	chosen - and what GetVideoSubtitle hands out is from it. The track
	already chosen is left as it is.

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: yes, on the file - and for a piece of sound being decoded by
	  another task to be done.
	- Interrupts: no.
	- Locks: no spinlock may be held.
	- Process: a process: the file is read.

   OWNERSHIP
	Called by the task that decodes the pictures. A program that plays the
	sound on a task of its own stops it first - the rate and the channels
	may change - and starts it again after.

   NOTES
	None.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`GetVideoTrack`, `GetVideoSubtitle`, `DecodeVideoSound`, `VCA_SoundTrack`,
	`VCA_Subtitles`

   EXAMPLES
	    sound.stop();
	    if (vcb.SelectVideoTrack(film, chosen, clock.now()) == vc.VCERR_OK) {
	        vcb.GetVideoStreamInfo(film, &info);
	    }
	    sound.restart(&info);
videocodec.library/VideoErrorText            videocodec.library/VideoErrorText

   NAME
	VideoErrorText -- Says what an error code means, in words.

   SYNOPSIS
	    fn VideoErrorText(base: *VideoCodecBase, code: i32) [*:0]const u8

   SINCE
	1.0. LVO -52.

   INPUTS
	- `code`: a VCERR_* code.

   RESULT
	A sentence fragment, lower case, no full stop: "nothing here decodes
	its pictures".

   BEHAVIOR
	A code it does not know is "unknown error".

   CONTEXT
	- Waits: no.
	- Interrupts: yes.
	- Locks: none.
	- Process: a task will do.

   OWNERSHIP
	The text is the library's, and does not change.

   NOTES
	None.

   BUGS
	None known.

   SEE ALSO
	`OpenVideoStream`, `DecodeVideoFrame`

   EXAMPLES
	    _ = Printf(dl, "%s\n", .{vcb.VideoErrorText(err)});
